Size the pusher's wait for the lease against the lease, not against a
constant.

A pusher that finds the writer lease held retries until the store lets
it in, bounded, because a lease that never expires is a successor
renewing rather than a predecessor winding down. The bound was sixty
seconds, chosen as four times the fifteen second lease a busy project
holds.

The ttl is not one number. A project nobody is writing backs its lease
off to five minutes, on purpose, so an idle tenant is not paying for a
renewal every fifteen seconds. A node that is killed while its project
is idle therefore leaves an expiry five minutes out, and the successor
gives up after sixty seconds of a wait that needed three hundred. It
stops its own postmaster, the supervisor starts another one, and that
one gives up after sixty seconds too. The store is unattachable until
some restart happens to land after the expiry, which on the soak was a
node that never came back at all: iteration 8, kill the whole node and
attach fresh, VIOLATION, server never became writable.

The number the wait needs is in the store already. The refusal carries
the expiry the lease was written with, and zou_wal_lease_held_until
now hands it to the waiter, so the bound is that expiry plus a few
seconds of clock grace, with the old sixty as a floor and as the whole
bound when the refusal reports nothing waitable.

The same number is what separates the two cases the bound was invented
to separate, which counting seconds never could. A holder that is gone
wrote its expiry once and nothing moves it again. A holder that is
alive writes it again every renewal. So an expiry seen to change is a
live node holding the store, and this stops on it at once instead of
waiting out a minute first, which is both correct and faster than what
it replaces.

See zou #1, the soak line.

